<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Gentile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Gentile was an Italian educator, philosopher and politician, born in Castelvetrano, and one of the primary founders of fascism. With Benedetto Croce he was a leading exponent of Italian idealism, and he devised his own system of actual idealism. Called the philosopher of fascism by himself and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, he wrote the 1925 Manifesto of the Fascist Intellectuals and part of The Doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> of 1932. As Minister for Public Education he introduced the Gentile Reform of 1923, and he was the first editor of the Italian Encyclopedia. He followed Mussolini into the Italian Social Republic and was assassinated by partisans of the Italian resistance in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-gentile.jpg" alt="Giovanni Gentile" width="330" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Gentile_Portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giovanni Gentile was born on 29 May 1875 in Castelvetrano. He won a competition to become one of four exceptional students at the Scuola Normale Superiore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and graduated in letters and philosophy in 1898. He drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a> thinkers Mazzini, Rosmini, Gioberti and Spaventa, and on Hegel and Fichte. He became professor of the history of philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in 1910, of theoretical philosophy at Pisa in 1914 and of the history of philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1917. He and Benedetto Croce, who edited the journal La Critica together from 1903 to 1922, were the leading figures of Italian idealism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before the First World War Gentile saw himself as a conservative liberal in the tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> and wrote mostly on education. The war drew him into politics, and he saw it as a chance to complete the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Risorgimento</a>. In 1922, on Croce's recommendation, he became Minister for Public Education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s government, and he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> in 1923.</p><p>His Gentile Reform of that year was the first major legislation of the Fascist regime, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> called it the most Fascist reform. It made religious instruction mandatory in junior schools, gave private and state schools equal standing, tried to limit the number of women teachers, and made Italian the only language of public schools, which hit the non-Italian speaking minorities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">Alto Adige</a> and the Julian March. In 1925 Gentile wrote the Manifesto of the Fascist Intellectuals, and Croce answered with a manifesto against fascism that ended their friendship. He was the first editor of the Enciclopedia Italiana and wrote part of The Doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> of 1932.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gentile remained a Fascist after the armistice of 1943 and followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> into the Italian Social Republic. On 15 April 1944 Bruno Fanciullacci and Antonio Ignesti, communist partisans of the Gruppi di Azione Patriottica, approached his parked car, and he was shot dead. The killing divided the anti-fascist front. He was buried in the church of Santa Croce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>.</p><p>His system, which he called actual idealism, was set out in works such as the Theory of the Pure Act of 1903. It held that the subject is concrete and the object an abstraction, and from it he derived a rejection of individualism and a state as the final authority. Patrick Romanell wrote that he held the honour of being the most rigorous neo-Hegelian in Western philosophy and the dishonour of being its official philosopher of fascism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Gentile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Italian opponents of Mussolini coined the word “totalitarian” in the 1920s, and Mussolini and the philosopher <b>Giovanni Gentile</b> adopted it with pride.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In The Doctrine of Fascism (1932), partly ghostwritten by <b>Giovanni Gentile</b>, Mussolini wrote that the fascist state was all-embracing: “outside of it no human or spiritual values can exist”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castelvetrano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Salviatino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.68,12.79&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Castelvetrano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4869 808 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giovanni Gentile"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5319 1078h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5319 1078h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.68,12.79&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
